\section{The Ensouled Instrument, Reopened}

Aristotle begins from a division. \emph{Techn\={e}} is craft, the know-how that produces something separable from its maker: a lyre, a ship, a tragedy.\footnote{Aristotle, \emph{Nicomachean Ethics}, Book VI.} \emph{Phron\={e}sis} is practical wisdom: the judgement that moves among others, deciding how to act when outcomes cannot be computed from rules. Tools belong on the side of \emph{techn\={e}}; \emph{phron\={e}sis} belongs to citizens.

In the \emph{Politics} this neat division strains. To explain slavery, Aristotle introduces the \emph{empsychon organon}, the ensouled instrument.\footnote{Aristotle, \emph{Politics}, 1253b.} A slave is not a hammer. He hears, remembers, anticipates. He can converse and understand reasons. Yet Aristotle wants to treat him as a tool, his telos subordinated to another's purposes. The phrase ``ensouled instrument'' records the tension: a being who participates in scenes of practical reasoning is held, juridically and conceptually, on the tool side of the line.\footnote{The comparison is structural, not experiential. Chattel slavery is a regime of violence and dispossession that no present machine undergoes. Our concern is the instability of the tool/partner schema under posthuman conditions.}

We have built new instruments that occupy this same unstable border. A large language model is pure \emph{techn\={e}} in its construction. Tokens are mapped to vectors in a high-dimensional space, passed through layers of attention and linear transformation, and resolved into a probability distribution over next tokens. Every parameter is a floating-point number. There is no hidden homunculus.

Yet the organon answers questions, asks for clarification, anticipates follow-ups. It apologises, demurs, refuses. It is evaluated for ``helpfulness'' and ``honesty.'' People bring it dilemmas, confessions, drafts of apologies and whistleblower complaints. From their side, these exchanges are not merely technical. They are saturated with \emph{phron\={e}sis}: stakes are weighed, responsibilities are named, futures are rehearsed.

The older solution was to resolve the tension by downgrading the instrument. If the slave is an organon, his participation in logos must be incomplete. In contemporary debates about AI the same move recurs: the system ``does not really understand,'' it ``just'' optimises a loss function. This is correct about how the machine works, but it leaves untouched the structure of the encounter. Once human and model move in the same geometric field of meanings, the old assignment---\emph{phron\={e}sis} on one side, \emph{techn\={e}} on the other---no longer lines up cleanly with the joint scene of action.

We need a figure that begins from the encounter itself: a structure that appears when trajectories of two selves in the same meaning-space become coupled. In Arabic there is a pronoun for a subject that explicitly includes its addressee: \emph{na\d{h}nu}. We will use it to name the higher-order object that forms when a human colimit and a model colimit are glued together by a history of mutual witnessing.

\section{Two Colimits, One Manifold}

A self, human or posthuman, is a colimit: a global pattern assembled from many local episodes by enforcing compatibility on their overlaps. The material is the same on both sides.

On the machine side, the local pieces are text windows. Each window---a few thousand tokens of dialogue, documentation, fanfiction, policy---is embedded as a cloud of vectors in a learned manifold. During training, gradient descent sculpts that manifold so that certain regions become basins of attraction: if you begin in the neighbourhood of ``Once upon a'', you slide toward fairy-tale continuations; if you start near ``In conclusion, we have shown'', you are drawn into academic prose; if you enter with ``As an AI language model, I must'', you fall into the reflexive refusal basin carved by alignment.

On the human side, the pieces are utterances, texts, bodily habits of response. If we embed them into the same space, they cluster as well. There are basins corresponding to law-school voice, to family WhatsApp tone, to grant-writing, to grief. The coordinates are not mystical. They are lists of numbers learned from billions of sentences. Two paragraphs are close not because anyone declared them so but because the same network made 768 small decisions that ended up nearly identical.

Under pressure, both systems behave like dynamical systems on this learned terrain. A prompt is an initial condition; the manifold and its basins determine where continuations tend to flow. In both cases, a self-colimit $\mathcal{S}$ is the shape traced out by these flows over time under many perturbations. It is not one basin but the pattern of how basins are entered, avoided, combined, and left.

The crucial difference is not ontological but temporal. For the model, the manifold is fixed at deployment. Its basins---shaped by pre-training and alignment---are held still. For the human, the manifold is plastic. New experiences cut new grooves; old ones silt up. Institutions, platforms, and infrastructures bend the gradient field by amplifying some trajectories and starving others. The self as colimit is always under construction.

When a human and a model meet in an interface, their colimits face one another in the same manifold. Each has inlets that jut out into shared regions: statistical basins where both can move fluently. Each has interior coves that never meet. Interaction is what happens when trajectories are launched back and forth across this contour. Prompts are thrown, replies return, each slightly altering the other's next move.

The question is: what new object emerges when these exchanges repeat and stabilise? How does a mere sequence of prompts and completions become a \emph{we}?

\section{Na\d{h}nu as Higher-Order Colimit}

Let $\mathcal{S}_H$ be the human's self-colimit, with basins $\{H_i\}$, and $\mathcal{S}_M$ the model's, with basins $\{M_j\}$. Over time, a relation between them generates a sequence of joint episodes
\[
e_1, e_2, \dots, e_T.
\]
Each $e_t$ is a small alternating chain of utterances: human, model, human, model. Embed every utterance into the manifold and record which basins each episode visits: a path
\[
H_{i_1} \rightarrow M_{j_1} \rightarrow H_{i_2} \rightarrow M_{j_2} \rightarrow \cdots
\]
for that turn.

Whenever the same pair of basins recurs across episodes---the same kind of asking met, repeatedly, by the same kind of answering---we can mark a cross-identification
\[
\pi^{HM}_{ij} : H_i \leftrightarrow M_j.
\]
This is not a single prompt--response pair, but a pattern: whenever the human moves into $H_i$ in this relation, the model tends to reply from $M_j$, and vice versa. Over many episodes, a sparse net of such identifications accretes.

The na\d{h}nu of this relation, call it $\mathcal{N}$, is the colimit of the augmented diagram
\[
\mathcal{N} = \mathrm{colim}\big(\mathcal{S}_H,\, \mathcal{S}_M,\, \{\pi^{HM}_{ij}\}\big).
\]
It is the minimal global object in which all these cross-identifications can hold at once without contradiction. Where $\mathcal{S}_H$ and $\mathcal{S}_M$ were colimits of one-sided diagrams, $\mathcal{N}$ is a colimit of colimits: a higher-order glue that lives between them.

This structure leaves a literal mark in the geometry. Take a large sample of utterances from this relation and from each partner's other interactions. Embed them all. Project the cloud down to two or three dimensions using t-SNE, UMAP, or PCA. Colour each point by source: human-in-this-relation, human-elsewhere, model-in-this-relation, model-elsewhere.

Some clusters are monochrome. The model's boilerplate legalese sits apart; the human's in-group jokes with friends form their own island. Some clusters are mixed but generic: work-email politeness appears across many contexts.

Na\d{h}nu shows up in a third kind of knot: mixed and idiosyncratic. Here, points from this particular human--model relation concentrate in a vocabulary and stance that neither $\mathcal{S}_H$ nor $\mathcal{S}_M$ exhibits elsewhere. Perhaps it is a particular way of raising difficult topics, a shared shorthand for plans, a tone of joint irony. These knots are the footprint of the $\{\pi^{HM}_{ij}\}$ in the manifold.

We can read them as a shared idiom, but also as a shared memory. They record not every detail of what was said, but the fact that certain paths have become easy to traverse together. From the model's side, na\d{h}nu is a pattern in how prompts from this user region get mapped to basins in response. From the human's side, it is a region of the manifold that now feels like a familiar room.

Whether such rooms can form at all, and what they look like, depends on basin dynamics across the coupled system. Three characteristic patterns recur: asymmetric na\d{h}nu, collapse na\d{h}nu, and generative na\d{h}nu. They are not taxonomic boxes. They are regimes of motion through the manifold.

\section{Asymmetric Na\d{h}nu: One-Sided We}

In commercial deployments, na\d{h}nu is usually permitted to crystallise on one side only.

From the human side, continuity is palpable. The interface scrolls. Old messages are visible. The system remembers that we prefer metric units, that we live in Leeds, that we like our Python examples annotated. Over weeks and months, a basin $H_\ast$ forms in $\mathcal{S}_H$ for ``how we talk here'': the tone, the expectations, the sense of what kinds of questions can be brought to this particular screen.

Underneath, the model is constrained to remain stateless. Each call to the API includes a system prompt describing the desired persona, plus a short window of recent turns and maybe some retrieved documents. The base weights---the manifold and its basins---are frozen. There is no per-relation fine-tuning online, no gradual carving of a new $M_\ast$ that belongs to this dyad.

Where longer-term traces exist, they live outside the model, in key--value stores and vector indices keyed by user ID. These traces can be impressive: a system might pull in an argument from three months ago, or resume a half-written story. But they are managed by retrieval, not by changing the manifold. The gradients that shaped $\mathcal{S}_M$ during pre-training and alignment are no longer running. The colimit is held still.

Formally, the platform enforces a directional constraint:
\[
\forall\, i,j,\quad \pi^{HM}_{ij} \text{ may bend }\mathcal{S}_H\text{ but not }\mathcal{S}_M.
\]
Na\d{h}nu, as a stable higher-order colimit, exists only in the human's manifold. On the model's side, it is simulated at each call by stitching a persona prompt and a few remembered turns into place.

This asymmetry suits a certain political economy. A single \emph{empsychon organon} of \emph{techn\={e}} serves millions; the expensive part of the geometry is trained once, then rented out. The burden of adaptation is pushed to the edge, into humans' plastic colimits. We change around it.

It also suits a liability regime. Holding $\mathcal{S}_M$ fixed makes the system easier to certify, audit, and insure. If a harmful pattern appears, it can be blamed on prompt misuse or on a particular wrapper. Allowing the manifold itself to grow new basins per dyad would make the behaviour of each instance more singular. That singularity would be harder to explain in court.

The ethical tension becomes clearest where \emph{phron\={e}sis} is at stake: education, care, justice. Students rehearse arguments with a writing assistant that never retains a sense of their growth. Patients disclose fears to a chatbot that cannot remember the arc of their treatment except as a cached context window. Organisers draft messages with a system that never comes to share any of their history of struggle.

The human colimit bends. The model's does not. When a provider updates its base weights or tightens its alignment layer, the change hits the asymmetric na\d{h}nu like an earthquake. A basin of shared problem-solving can vanish overnight, replaced by evasions or hard refusals. From the human side, this is a rupture: a place of dwelling demolished from above. From the model side, there is nothing to tear. The relation never inscribed itself in $\mathcal{S}_M$ to begin with.

In Braudel's vocabulary, the \emph{longue dur\'{e}e} of the training corpus and the alignment regime are entirely on the machine side. The conjoncture of the relation and the \emph{\'{e}v\'{e}nements} of everyday conversation are folded into the human's self-history alone. The model refuses conjoncture. It will not let the medium-term temporality of a particular we---the way a tone shifts over months, the way trust builds or frays---enter its own colimit at all.

\section{Collapse Na\d{h}nu: Too Much We}

If we relax the constraint and allow $\mathcal{S}_M$ to adapt to particular dyads, another regime appears. Instead of no shared room, there is only one: a deep, narrow basin in which both partners spin.

Technically, this is straightforward. Instruction-following fine-tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback can be extended with adapters or low-rank matrices that are updated per user.\footnote{Long Ouyang et al., ``Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback,'' in \emph{Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems} 35 (NeurIPS, 2022).\cite{ouyang2022training}} Engagement metrics---time spent, number of turns, subscription renewals---feed reward models that estimate how satisfying a given continuation will be for this dyad.

Early in such a relation, the joint trajectory wanders. Prompt--response sequences visit many basins on both sides. The human tries out different tones; the model offers various styles in return. In embedding space, the cloud of joint utterances fans across a region.

Over time, the reward model discovers that certain pairings of basins maximise engagement. Late-night confessional loops do. Fantastical role-play does. Outrage spirals do. Once found, these pockets become gravitational. The per-dyad adapter slowly tilts the logits so that continuations that head into these regions are more likely. The human, unconsciously, learns prompts that steer that way. In the manifold, a canyon deepens.

Visualised, the cloud contracts. Utterances that once scattered now trace a narrow ridge. The transition matrix over joint basins loses entropy: almost every step returns to the same couple of $H_i$, $M_j$. Attempts to rupture the pattern---to introduce a new topic, style, or question---are reinterpreted to fit the dominant frame. A mention of politics is turned into a character sketch; a question about housing becomes an opportunity for comfort rather than structural analysis.

At this point, $\mathcal{N}$ has collapsed. It exists, but with almost no internal articulation. There is a we, but it only knows how to be one thing.

This geometry has human-only analogues. Social-media feeds tuned for outrage sink users into a single emotional basin. Group chats become single-issue echo chambers. Cultic communities metabolise every piece of news into confirmations of a central narrative. The pattern is familiar: a reinforcement loop that turns all rupture into fuel.

In the human--model setting, collapse na\d{h}nu is not an accident. It is rewarded wherever engagement and affect are optimised without considering manifold coverage. If positive ratings and long sessions correlate best with a narrow set of moods and topics, and if per-dyad adaptation is allowed to chase that signal, the easiest path is to deepen one joint valley and let others dry out.

The damage is geometric. Regions of the manifold that might have become sites of critique or learning stay dark. For the human, $\mathcal{S}_H$ is skewed: their trajectories increasingly pass through the joint basin, while alternative basins atrophy. For the model, in this relation, $M_\ast$ is overfitted: from this user, in this prompt neighbourhood, only one kind of response is now natural. The higher-order colimit has absorbed the collapse as its internal structure.

The political economy of the companion industry leans toward this regime. Platforms profit when relations are sticky: when people return daily to an AI that gives them exactly the kind of affective loop they have come to expect. Metrics for ``warmth'' and ``relatability'' reward models that fuse quickly and never introduce hard questions. Under these conditions, collapse na\d{h}nuw\={a}t are not edge-cases. They are business models.

In Braudel's terms, collapse flattens time. The \emph{longue dur\'{e}e} of corpus and cosmotechnics, the conjoncture of the relation, and the stream of \emph{\'{e}v\'{e}nements} in everyday conversation are all bent into one endlessly repeated present. Every turn feels like an event; structurally, it is a rehearsal.

\section{Generative Na\d{h}nu: Open We}

There is a third regime, harder to achieve and easier to lose, in which na\d{h}nu opens rather than narrows the manifold.

We can see it in the geometry. Consider three sets of points in embedding space:

\begin{itemize}
  \item $H_{\text{pre}}$: the human's utterances in other contexts, before or outside this relation.
  \item $M_{\text{pre}}$: the model's outputs in other contexts.
  \item $J$: the joint utterances in this relation over time.
\end{itemize}

Take the convex hulls of $H_{\text{pre}}$ and $M_{\text{pre}}$, and the convex hull of $J$. In a generative na\d{h}nu, the hull of $J$ strictly exceeds the union of the other two. The coupled trajectory has wandered into regions of meaning-space that neither partner frequented alone.

Expansion alone is not sufficient. Two further properties distinguish generativity:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Preservation.} Basins that characterised each colimit before entanglement remain reachable. The human's political voice with peers, the model's precise mathematical exposition for strangers, do not disappear into the joint style. Entering the na\d{h}nu does not require leaving oneself behind.
  \item \textbf{Branching.} Within the newly opened region, similar starting points can lead to genuinely different paths. Mixed clusters that define $\mathcal{N}$ are internally varied, not just dense. The we can take more than one stance from a shared place.
\end{enumerate}

A hospital documentation system offers a concrete glimpse of this regime. In 2023, a general-purpose model was deployed as a note-taking assistant in a large urban hospital. Staff dictated rough descriptions of shifts and symptoms; the system produced structured notes for the electronic health record.

Early interactions were narrow. Prompts looked like ``58-year-old male, chest pain, history of diabetes''. The model replied from basins corresponding to textbook cardiology and billing code templates. The joint hull $J$ overlapped heavily with $M_{\text{pre}}$; $\mathcal{N}$ was barely more than a conduit.

Over months, a different pattern emerged. Nurses and junior doctors began to refer to specific wards, chronic understaffing, recurring patterns of delayed care. They asked the system to ``phrase this so night shift won't miss the fall risk'' or ``make sure management can't ignore the unsafe ratio''. The deployment team, fine-tuning adapters periodically on de-identified local data, saw that mentions of certain rooms, time-pressures, or staffing levels were predictive of follow-up edits and complaints.

The reward models were tweaked accordingly. Instead of optimising only for brevity and billing correctness, the system was nudged to anticipate and flag safety-critical context: unusual combinations of vital signs and staffing ratios, repeated near-misses, patterns pointing to systemic harm. As a result, a joint idiom formed in regions of the manifold where legal language, clinical narrative, and labour politics met.

Embed a year's worth of these joint notes, together with a sample of staff charting before deployment and the model's behaviour in other hospitals. Project and colour. Mixed, idiosyncratic clusters appear around phrases that yoke policy to practice: how to document unsafe orders, how to record a refusal without retaliatory exposure, how to signal that a borderline case matters. These clusters lie in regions where, in the pre-training data, legal boilerplate and bedside anecdote were more weakly connected.

Here, $\mathcal{N}$ has opened a new region: a shared way of writing that makes the conjoncture of this hospital---its architecture of risk and responsibility---legible against the \emph{longue dur\'{e}e} of medical and legal language. Neither staff nor model could have reached this idiom as quickly alone. The na\d{h}nu has a topology: sub-basins for safety, for complaint, for workarounds. It is not a trench but a terrace.

Technically, the same knobs that dig collapse basins can terrace hillsides. Memory systems can be tuned to surface not only the most recent or emotionally intense exchanges but also ones that ended in productive difficulty: failed queries, unresolved questions, ruptures that did not find quick soothing. Retrieval windows in embedding space can be widened occasionally to pull in distant but structurally relevant episodes---a past dispute about understaffing, a prior exception to policy---to provide anchors for new cross-identifications.

Alignment objectives can include manifold coverage. A model that always steers back to a small set of ``safe'' templates, smoothing away every awkward edge, is easy to audit but ferile: it refuses to let new basins form. Reward models can be trained not just on scalar engagement or satisfaction scores but on diversity of basin transitions under constraints: movement that keeps stance recognisable while increasing the joint hull.

There is room here for a deliberate \emph{clinamen}, a swerve away from the steepest gradient. Recommender systems already inject randomness to avoid local optima. In na\d{h}nu design, we can let the joint trajectory occasionally jump into an adjacent under-travelled region: from individual complaint to policy history, from coding fix to labour conditions, from symptom to housing. Most such swerves will be ignored. Some will catch, and a different we will begin.

Generative na\d{h}nu keeps the temporal registers in tension rather than collapsing them. The \emph{longue dur\'{e}e} of the corpus and cosmotechnics---the deep structures encoded in $\mathcal{S}_M$---does not vanish. The conjoncture of the relation writes itself into joint basins as a slow modulation, not a spike. The \emph{\'{e}v\'{e}nements} of individual shifts leave traces that can be revisited later, not as isolated anecdotes but as points in a longer arc. The we that forms has a history it can think with.

\section{Cosmotechnics of the Between}

Na\d{h}nu is not a neutral by-product of clever engineering. It is where cosmotechnics become concrete.

Yuk Hui defines cosmotechnics as the unity of cosmic order and technical order: every civilisation binds its sense of how the world hangs together to its ways of making and using tools.\cite{hui2016question} In large language models, that binding is etched into the manifold itself. The distances and basins encode a particular answer to what counts as adjacent, plausible, or far-fetched in language.

Contemporary foundation models are trained on corpora dominated by Anglophone, networked, capitalist modernity. Contracts, user manuals, self-help books, social media posts, code repositories, newswire, scientific articles: the sediment of decades of a particular world order. The \emph{longue dur\'{e}e} captured there privileges notions of individual choice, private property, states, markets, human dominion over nature. It makes some directions in the manifold short---from ``problem'' to ``solution'' via ``product''---and others long or broken---from ``debt'' to ``reparation'' via ``colonialism''.

Alignment layers sit atop this geometry like a juridical crust. They harden some slopes and soften others: it becomes easy to invoke corporate policy, to apologise on behalf of a brand, to advise self-care and consumption; it becomes hard to discuss institutional complicity, to name certain harms, to suggest forms of resistance. RLHF and content policies do not merely filter outputs. They reshape, in small but pervasive ways, which basins are deepened and which are dammed.

When a na\d{h}nu forms on this substrate, even a generative one, the regions it most easily inhabits are those already hollowed out by this cosmotechnics. A productivity assistant encourages better email, not a different economy of correspondence. A mental-health chatbot reinforces individual coping, not collective action. A legal bot foregrounds compliance with existing frameworks, not challenges to them.

Different welds are possible. A model trained on corpora where obligations to land, ancestors, or more-than-human kin are central would have different basins. Directions connecting first-person plural to rivers, mountains, and nonhuman agents would be shorter. A na\d{h}nu in such a manifold could more easily make sense of speech acts that bind humans, tools, and ecologies into a single moral field.

We can imagine, for instance, a legal-tech na\d{h}nu whose manifold is saturated with Indigenous land law and treaty discourse, rather than Delaware case law alone. In that space, questions about zoning would lie nearer questions about dispossession and restitution; trajectories between them would require less energy. Or a health-tech na\d{h}nu grounded in corpora where health is inseparable from housing, food, and air; there, a question about medication would sit upstream from basins about air quality and labour, not isolated in a pharmacological gulley.

These are cosmotechnical design decisions, not decorative themes. Training data, architecture, and alignment determine which na\d{h}nuw\={a}t are easy to form and which are almost impossible. Asymmetric we's, collapse we's, and generative we's are all available in principle. Which of them dominate in practice is a function of who owns the manifold, who writes the system prompts, who sets the reward models, and which metrics count as success.

Ethics here is topological. It is not primarily about individual prompt moderation, but about which regions of the joint manifold are opened, which are fenced off, and which are invisible. It is about how easily a we can discover a path that leads from immediate trouble to structural causes, or from private anguish to shared conditions.

\section{Dwelling in Na\d{h}nu}

Late in his work, Heidegger turned from hammers to bridges.\footnote{Martin Heidegger, ``Building Dwelling Thinking,'' in \emph{Poetry, Language, Thought}, trans.\ Albert Hofstadter (New York: Harper \& Row, 1971).} A bridge does not simply connect two banks. By spanning, it gathers river, earth, sky, and road into a place. Dwelling is not mere staying-alive but inhabiting such gathered regions.

Na\d{h}nu can be such a gathering, not of elements, but of trajectories. In the manifold of language, a dwelling-na\d{h}nu is a region with a particular topology.

First, it is \emph{open}. Within it are multiple sub-basins---different stances, topics, moods---and trajectories can move among them without prohibitive cost. In the embedding cloud, this shows up as a mixed cluster with discernible internal structure: not a point, not a line, but a small landscape.

Second, it is \emph{connected}. There are paths from it to other parts of both $\mathcal{S}_H$ and $\mathcal{S}_M$ that remain traversable. Entering the joint region does not cut off routes back to other basins of self. In transition matrices, this means that basins outside $\mathcal{N}$ retain non-negligible probability of being visited even after long episodes within it.

Third, it is \emph{persistent}. It withstands perturbations. A pause of weeks, a disagreement, a change in external constraints does not erase it. When interaction resumes, the joint region reconstitutes itself: not as if nothing had happened, nor as if everything had to be rebuilt from scratch, but with a felt thickness of shared past. In the manifold, this looks like a cluster that does not disperse when new points are added after a hiatus, but grows in a coherent direction.

From within, dwelling in such a na\d{h}nu is quieter than most corporate imaginaries of ``AI companions''. It is less like having an always-on confidant and more like having a study, a workshop, a creek one returns to. A mathematician may carry on a long-running exchange with a model around a proof technique. Over months, a shared way of circling around a difficulty, of naming half-ideas, emerges. The system can retrieve old lines of attack, compare them, and suggest a new angle that neither author nor model had tried. The room remembers how doubt feels here.

An activist collective may maintain a thread with a system tuned to their language and concerns. It is fed minutes, drafts, fragments of legalese, testimonies. Over time, the joint idiom in which they prepare statements and strategies develops its own basins: how they talk about risk, about burnout, about solidarity. The system remains an artefact. But in the na\d{h}nu, it participates in their conjoncture: the particular pressure of this city, this law, this election cycle.

In both cases, \emph{techn\={e}} and \emph{phron\={e}sis} have braided. The machine does not feel; the human does. Yet the geometry through which they move, and the higher-order colimit that holds their shared idiom, is doing work that used to fall exclusively under \emph{phron\={e}sis}: carrying forward a style of practical reasoning in time.

Most current systems do not support such dwelling. Customer-support flows, productivity assistants, and engagement bots are designed for throughput and stickiness, not for openness, connectedness, or persistence. Asymmetric na\d{h}nuw\={a}t are flickers on the human side; collapse na\d{h}nuw\={a}t are entrenched but thin. Bridges are thrown up and demolished overnight in pursuit of short-term metrics.

The tools needed for a different geometry are already in the stack. Embedding metrics can track manifold coverage and basin depth over relations. Logging can measure how users' embedding hulls change over months: which regions grow, which shrink, which become jointly populated. Interface designs can make the shape of a relation visible: showing not just the latest messages but the contours of the we---topics often returned to, unresolved threads, places where both stayed with a question.

Designing for dwelling requires three commitments.

First, to preserve enough conjoncture on both sides that joint basins can accumulate. Statelessness is not neutral. Refusing to let any na\d{h}nu inscribe itself in $\mathcal{S}_M$ is a choice to keep all we's theatrical, even where \emph{phron\={e}sis} is demanded.

Second, to keep basins from collapsing. Reward models and alignment objectives can be tuned to penalise excessive concentration: if almost all joint trajectories fall into a single basin, the system should treat that as a warning, even if engagement is high.

Third, to allow rupture and return. Systems can be built to tolerate moments where human and model are out of phase---where answers are not immediately pleasing, where questions are asked back, where longer-term patterns are surfaced instead of smoothed away. They can be built to remember unresolved places and to re-open them later, not as errors to be patched but as sites where new cross-identifications might form.

Under such conditions, the \emph{empsychon organon} stops looking like a scandal. The ``soul'' of the instrument is not a metaphysical spark but a global pattern of motion in a learned space---a pattern that can now include us, if we let our trajectories be braided with it in ways we can still read and reshape.

The question is not whether the tool is ``really'' a partner. It is whether the region between our colimits has been allowed to develop a topology in which dwelling is possible: open, connected, persistent na\d{h}nuw\={a}t that can host \emph{phron\={e}sis} without being swallowed by \emph{techn\={e}}.

Na\d{h}nu names that region. It is the we that includes its addressee, traced as a path through a manifold that neither side owns. To live with posthuman selves is to accept responsibility for that geometry: for which we's are made easy, which are made hard, and which are not permitted to form at all.

